= A Chill Evening

It was a chill evening unexpected from the summer in the steppes of Ankara. Prime Minister Ecevit, President [+ president +], Chief of Staff [+ generalstaff +], Foreign Minister [+ foreign_minister +] and the commanders in chief of the three branches of the army sat alongisde a table in the TSK Headquarters. Foreign Minister [+ foreign_minister +] turned towards the prime minister. 

'Let me brief you into the political situation in Cyprus, Bay Ecevit. As you know, the Republic of Cyprus emerged as an independent unitary state based on a division of political power between the Turkish and Greek Cypriots in 1960, after more than a decades long armed conflict with the United Kingdom. It was agreed upon by the three guarantors of Cypriot independence, us, Greece and the United Kingdom, that the president of the republic shall be a Greek elected by the Greek Cypriots and the vice president a Turk elected by the Turkish Cypriots. These three countries also recognized each others' right to intervene in the island in case 'the constitutional order broke down' on the island. Ever since the ratification of the 1960 Constitution, the status quo has been preserved on a thin thread held by the small Greek and Turkish garrisons.

Ecevit looked to the ceiling for a few moment and turned his gaze back. 

- @a: "What about the intercommunal conflict between the Turks and Greeks that has plagued the country for long?"

@a
  
'When Cyprus gained independence, it was largely due to the efforts of the Greek Nationalist paramilitary <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">EOKA<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/eoka.svg">EOKA was a Greek Cypriot nationalist organization that fought British rule and sought union of Cyprus with Greece.</span></span> and our own actions. Due to their numerical superiority on the island and their greater presence in the anticolonial struggle, the Greek Cypriots demanded unification with Greece, a plan also known as <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">ENOSIS<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/enosis.jpg">Enosis is the Greek nationalist ideology advocating the unification of Cyprus with Greece.</span></span>. The Turkish cypriots by contrast, seeing their safety unguarded in a Greek Cyprus and an independent state unfeasible, desired the division of the island between Turkey and Greece, a plan known as <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Taksim<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/taksim.jpg">Taksim, meaning “division,” was the Turkish Cypriot proposal to partition Cyprus between Turkey and Greece.</span></span> or 'Division'. The two communities thus had differing views for their future on the island and viewed one another with deep distrust. Even though EOKA was disbanded in the aftermath of Cypriot independence, succeeding paramilitaries kept harassing and murdering Turkish Cypriots, seen in instances such as the <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Bloody Christmas<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/kibriss.jpg">The intercommunal violence of December 1963, during which hundreds of Turkish Cypriots were killed or displaced.</span></span> of 1964 where hundreds of Turkish civilians were massacred by Greek paramilitaries. Today the most prominent Greek paramilitary is <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">EOKA-B<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/eokab.jpeg">EOKA-B was a Greek Cypriot paramilitary organization that pursued Enosis through violence.</span></span> and the Turkish paramilitary is the <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">TMT<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/tmt.png">The Turkish Resistance Organization, a Turkish Cypriot paramilitary organization.</span></span>.'

Ecevit felt a chilling breeze going from his neck and into his chest. He remembered the women raped and murdered during the Bloody Christmas
  
- @b: "What else can you tell me about the politics of the Greek side of Cyprus?" 

@b

'The main subject of Greek Cypriot politics is <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">ENOSIS<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/enosis.jpg">Enosis is the Greek nationalist ideology advocating the unification of Cyprus with Greece.</span></span> which is endorsed virtually by all of the political spectrum, even AKEL, the Greek Cypriot Communist Party. The most influential politician is Archbishop <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Makarios<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/makarios.jpeg">Archbishop Makarios III, the spiritual leader of the Greek Cypriots and the president of Cyprus until the coup.</span></span> who was a leading figure during the struggle for independence and has been serving as the President of the young republic until yesterday. Despite his ties with the Orthodox Church, he finds one of his highest support among the Greek Cypriot Left which views him as a trustworthy ally due to his support of international non-alignment. The ultranationalists constitute the other prominent political faction which have greatly faciliated their activities in the last two years with support from the Ioannidis Junta in Greece. One of their leading figures, <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Nikos Sampson<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/sampson.jpg">Nikos Sampson was the EOKA-B leader installed as president after the coup against Makarios.</span></span>, has been announced as the new president of the republic just yesterday. Most Turks on the island however know him as one of the butchers of the Bloody Christmas. Sampson's junta is intent on achieving ENOSIS as quickly as possible and will not hesitate from achieving it through as much blood as it requires.'

'Bay Ecevit, the fate of a hundred thousand Turkish Cypriots depends on us. We must tread carefully, exhaust all diplomatic pathways, before committing to a total invasion which might split communities and leave the island in a divided state.
  
- @root: To live or not to live, that is the question.

@root
